Notebook[{

Cell[CellGroupData[{
Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry", "GuideTitle",
 CellID->942062912,ExpressionUUID->"a3a3c038-ca24-47b7-9945-83f1c1312fe3"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["Reviewed by Pavel Hajek on 18 October 2026.", "TI"]], \
"GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["Euclidean Infrageometry studies geometric objects on graphs using synthetic methods. An object may have several realizations on a given graph. We study these families, their mutual relations, and constructions formed by successive operations.", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["If space is represented by a large graph, we may view it as a web of paths. Infinitesimality is an idealized abstraction of our large-scale perception of this web. This leads to the idealization of space as a “continuous web” supporting objects such as the “breadthless line.” Infrageometry works directly with the discrete substrate at a chosen observer scale and aggregates instead of subdivides.", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["A geometric object may have several realizations on a graph. We treat these realizations together as a multi-object. Choosing realizations at successive construction steps generates a branching multiway system. For a fixed construction scheme, the prescribed dependency graph is the same across branches, although the geometric realizations may differ. We call this fixed dependency structure causal invariance.", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["We associate each multi-object with vertex and edge densities by counting occurrences across its realizations. For a finite nonempty family, dividing by the number of realizations gives expected occupation under uniform sampling. Further normalization gives probability measures on vertices or edges. We ask when suitable scaling and assumptions on the graph sequence make these measures converge weakly to measures supported on classical “thin” geometric objects.", "GuideAbstract", CellID -> 818260801, ExpressionUUID -> "0438374e-dc24-4195-94e2-2eb28a4046df"],

Cell["We also seek an axiomatic system for Euclidean infrageometry and conditions defining Euclidean-like graph sequences. The aim is to recover the objects, relations and axioms of classical Euclidean geometry in an appropriate scaling limit.", "GuideAbstract", CellID -> 818260802, ExpressionUUID -> "6c6f1d63-c168-414b-9798-ef2d7dd224b6"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We use inert objects such as ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSegment"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSegment", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["q", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "422a2468-7402-4a07-a951-4398e07374c0"],
 ", independent of a graph substrate. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraMeasurement"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraMeasurement", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["obj", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["property", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "b1aa4ecd-cf7a-435a-aca5-4d63e3861090"],
 " then computes a property of an object on a concrete graph. Ambiguous \
objects also have named samplers; for example, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraSegment"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraSegment", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["g", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    RowBox[{"InfraSegment", "[", 
     RowBox[{
      StyleBox["p", "TI"], ",", " ", 
      StyleBox["q", "TI"]}], "]"}], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["k", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "41193634-3528-4928-ab2a-109e348227be"],
 " gives up to ",
 StyleBox["k", "TI"],
 " shortest paths from ",
 StyleBox["p", "TI"],
 " to ",
 StyleBox["q", "TI"],
 " as vertex lists."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell["\<\
Segments, rays, lines and arcs have compact representations as directed \
acyclic graphs such that the chains correspond to individual representatives. \
This allows densities to be computed efficiently without enumerating every \
path.\
\>", "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Inspired by ",
 ButtonBox["GeometricScene",
  BaseStyle->"Hyperlink",
  ButtonData->{
    URL["https://reference.wolfram.com/language/ref/GeometricScene.html"], 
    None}],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraScene"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraScene", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "81fe5292-3929-47e8-8dce-486083657c7e"],
 " lets us describe a construction through named objects and hypotheses. ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["RandomInfraSceneInstance"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/RandomInfraSceneInstance", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["scene", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["graph", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "78a2291e-079e-4fbc-84cf-8c2c3fd964cc"],
 " draws an admissible instance on a given substrate, and ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   TemplateBox[{
     Cell[
      TextData["InfraSceneViewer"]], 
     "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraSceneViewer", 
     "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
    "PackageLink",
    BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"], "[", 
   RowBox[{
    StyleBox["scene", "TI"], ",", " ", 
    StyleBox["graph", "TI"]}], "]"}]], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "0395e63d-114e-4861-8686-28c65df9eddf"],
 " displays the construction step by step in both the causal and branchial \
directions."
}], "GuideAbstract",
 CellID->2001916300,ExpressionUUID->"ef0ab37b-2e0d-4399-8f7e-82603ccbe925"]
}, Open  ]],

